--TEST--
$uri->sign answers the receiver with the reserved `_sig` set, $uri->verifySignature answers nothing, and adding, editing or dropping any parameter is one refusal — `rule:core-api/signing-is-over-a-payload`, `rule:core-api/one-refusal-except-expiry`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::sign, Core\Uri::verifySignature
// A signed URL is a `Uri`, not a string a caller assembles: it composes with
// `with`, `withQueryParameter` and `toString` like every other member here.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

Core\Uri $link = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/invoice?id=7&scope=own")
    ->sign({keys: $ring, until: null});

echo $link->host(), $link->path(), "\n";
echo $link->queryParameter("_sig") == null
    ? "no token"
    : "the reserved parameter carries the token", "\n";

// It answers nothing. There are no claims to hand back — the claim is the URL
// the caller is already holding — and a `bool` is a value a caller can drop on
// the floor, so reaching the next statement is what says the link is good.
$link->verifySignature($ring);
echo "verified", "\n";

// Every component present is covered, so appending a parameter is a different
// URL whatever it is called. This is the bypass every framework in this space
// has shipped as an option naming which parameters are signed, and there is no
// such option at any of the three doors.
try {
    Core\Uri::parse($link->toString() . "&admin=1")->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "an appended parameter accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $appended) {
    echo $appended->message, "\n";
}

// Dropping the parameter that *narrows* the link is as much a forgery as
// adding one, and the signature covers both directions.
try {
    $link->withQueryParameter("scope", null)->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "a removed parameter accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $removed) {
    echo $removed->message, "\n";
}

try {
    $link->withQueryParameter("id", "8")->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "an edited parameter accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $edited) {
    echo $edited->message, "\n";
}

// And the URL with no `_sig` at all — the code path a naive verifier treats as
// "no signature to check" — is the same sentence, because telling it apart is
// telling an attacker that stripping the parameter is the cheaper attack.
try {
    Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/invoice?id=7&scope=own")->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "an unsigned URL accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $missing) {
    echo $missing->message, "\n";
}

// The bound's other side: the untouched link still verifies, so the four
// refusals above are about their URLs and not about a member that refuses
// everything.
$link->verifySignature($ring);
echo "and the untouched link still verifies", "\n";
--EXPECT--
example.com/invoice
the reserved parameter carries the token
verified
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
and the untouched link still verifies
